event: response.reasoning_summary_text.done
data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done","sequence_number":200,"item_id":"rs_03b34ddd3a1b649f016aca1095afa081939b1ce7385a1e430d","output_index":0,"summary_index":1,"text":"**Enhancing task rendering**\n\nI need to stabilize task rendering using closures and Object.assign for reusability. My plan is to initialize rendering with a cached map of existing records. When constructing updates, I want to ensure that all saved properties differ from archived snapshots. If destination options change, I will rebuild rows accordingly. Using a fresh approach will help maintain the current state without memory leaks. To avoid unnecessary invalidation, I’ll ensure that cached records only refresh if the relevant signatures haven’t changed. This way, drafts are preserved while efficiently managing updates."}

event: response.reasoning_summary_part.done
data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_part.done","sequence_number":201,"item_id":"rs_03b34ddd3a1b649f016aca1095afa081939b1ce7385a1e430d","output_index":0,"summary_index":1,"part":{"type":"summary_text","text":"**Enhancing task rendering**\n\nI need to stabilize task rendering using closures and Object.assign for reusability. My plan is to initialize rendering with a cached map of existing records. When constructing updates, I want to ensure that all saved properties differ from archived snapshots. If destination options change, I will rebuild rows accordingly. Using a fresh approach will help maintain the current state without memory leaks. To avoid unnecessary invalidation, I’ll ensure that cached records only refresh if the relevant signatures haven’t changed. This way, drafts are preserved while efficiently managing updates."}}

